<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paranjothi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Siruthondar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paranjothi was a general of the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a> who led the army that captured and sacked the Chalukya capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a> in 642. He later renounced war and became a devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, revered as Siruthondar, one of the 63 Nayanar saints of Tamil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>, whose story is told in the twelfth-century Periya Puranam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paranjothi.jpg" alt="Paranjothi" width="320" height="716" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Itsmethunder9783 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:23ty_Picture.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
